"body": "## Routes\n- **System-generated**: subnet routes (one per subnet range) and a default route `0.0.0.0/0` to the internet gateway.\n- **Custom static routes**: next hop can be an instance, an internal passthrough load balancer (for HA appliances), a VPN tunnel, or an IP.\n- **Dynamic routes**: learned via BGP by **Cloud Router** (from VPN/Interconnect). VPC **dynamic routing mode**: regional or global.\n- Most specific prefix wins; ties broken by priority.\n\n## Private Google Access (PGA)\nEnabled per subnet. VMs with only internal IPs can reach Google APIs (Cloud Storage, BigQuery...) via the default internet route but without leaving Google's network. For on-prem hosts or stricter control, use special VIPs: `private.googleapis.com` or `restricted.googleapis.com` (for VPC Service Controls), with DNS and routes.\n\n## Cloud NAT\n- Managed, software-defined **outbound** NAT per region (attached to a Cloud Router). No NAT VMs to manage.\n- Lets private VMs, GKE nodes, Cloud Run (with VPC egress) reach the internet for updates and APIs.\n- **No inbound** connections are allowed through Cloud NAT.\n- Auto-allocated or manually reserved external IPs (useful when partners allowlist your egress IPs). Configure ports per VM and logging.",
"use": "Remove external IPs from VMs, enable PGA on subnets, and add Cloud NAT where outbound internet is needed. Use static routes with an internal load balancer next hop to send traffic through firewall appliances.",
"ex": "A payment service must call a partner API that allowlists source IPs. The VMs have no external IPs; Cloud NAT uses two reserved static IPs that the partner allowlists. Calls to Cloud Storage go via Private Google Access.",
"cmd": "gcloud compute routers create nat-router --network=prod-vpc --region=us-central1\ngcloud compute addresses create nat-ip-1 nat-ip-2 --region=us-central1\ngcloud compute routers nats create prod-nat --router=nat-router --region=us-central1 \\\n  --nat-all-subnet-ip-ranges --nat-external-ip-pool=nat-ip-1,nat-ip-2 --enable-logging\n\ngcloud compute networks subnets update prod-us-central1 --region=us-central1 \\\n  --enable-private-ip-google-access\ngcloud compute routes list --filter=\"network:prod-vpc\"",
