"flow": "1. Players log in and request a match through Cloud Run APIs; Open Match groups players by skill and region.\n2. Agones allocates a ready game server pod in the nearest regional GKE cluster; the client connects directly over UDP.\n3. Match results update Spanner (inventory, XP) transactionally and update Redis leaderboards.\n4. Game servers and clients emit telemetry to Pub/Sub, which lands in BigQuery for dashboards within minutes.",
"trade": "| Choice | Benefit | Cost / risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| Agones on GKE Standard | Fine control of node pools, host networking and fleets | Cluster operations are on the studio; Autopilot has constraints for some game server patterns |\n| Spanner for inventory | No item duplication, global scale | Schema design must avoid hotspots (no monotonically increasing keys) |\n| Regional Redis leaderboards | Very fast reads | Global leaderboard needs aggregation; Redis is a cache, rebuild from Spanner if lost |\n| Spot VMs for some game server node pools | Large savings | Preemption ends matches; use for lobbies or low-stakes modes only |",
"exam": "Game servers are stateful, latency-sensitive workloads; Kubernetes with Agones is the common pattern on Google Cloud. \"Global consistency for player data\" → Spanner. \"Leaderboard\" → Redis sorted sets. Primary keys that increase monotonically create Spanner hotspots.",
