"flow": "1. Users sign in through Identity Platform; each tenant has its own provider configuration, and tokens carry the tenant ID.\n2. The API layer enforces per-tenant quotas and routes enterprise tenants to their dedicated deployment.\n3. Pooled tenants share a database where every query is scoped by tenant ID with row-level security as a backstop.\n4. Enterprise tenants get a project in an EU region with a dedicated database encrypted with their own key; revoking key access renders the data unreadable.\n5. Labels (`tenant=...`) and billing export make per-tenant cost visible.",
"trade": "| Model | Benefit | Cost / risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pooled | Cheapest per tenant, simple ops | Noisy neighbors; a bug can leak across tenants without strict scoping |\n| Siloed (project per tenant) | Strong isolation, residency and per-tenant keys | Higher base cost, automation required for thousands of projects |\n| Hybrid tiers | Price isolation into enterprise plans | Two deployment shapes to test and operate |",
"exam": "Projects are the strongest common isolation boundary (IAM, quotas, billing, network). \"Customer can revoke access\" → CMEK under customer control (or Cloud External Key Manager for keys outside Google). \"SSO per tenant\" → Identity Platform multi-tenancy.",
